Use case: stylized-concept.
Asset type: production pixel-art animation spritesheet featuring the EXISTING characters Vanessa and her white Chihuahua Jerry.
Input image 1 is the definitive character identity, clothing, size and pixel-art reference. Preserve the SAME Vanessa: warm brown bob hair with copper highlights, big brown eyes, tiny subtle labret and small earrings, black jacket over white top, blue jeans, white-gray sneakers and black backpack. Preserve the SAME Jerry: small cream-white short-haired Chihuahua, huge upright triangular pink-inner ears, black eyes and tiny nose, curved tail, dark reddish collar; not a fluffy dog or teddy bear.
Canvas EXACTLY 1086 wide by 1448 high, unchanged from the character reference. THREE equal columns and FOUR equal rows, TWELVE 362x362 cells, no gutters, labels or drawn grid. These are 12 consecutive CHRONOLOGICAL frames read row-major, NOT rows of different directions.
Preserve approximately 320-pixel standing height for Vanessa and 140-pixel standing height for Jerry INCLUDING ears. Same character proportions, scale and square-pixel rendering in all frames. Show both ENTIRE characters in every cell with at least 12px clear alpha margin, no ears, tails, pointing hands, heads or feet clipped or crossing into adjacent cells.
One fixed front three-quarter view throughout. Vanessa's planted shoes remain at local x around 150 and baseline y=340 in every cell, with room to her RIGHT for Jerry and his action. Maintain Vanessa's position and scale; Jerry's movement is animated relative to her. Fully transparent surroundings, no scene, floor, ground patches, shadows, sparkles, UI, text or watermark. No extra limbs or characters.
Only use input image 1 for this animation. Ignore the toy references: no toy in this sheet.
Create a coherent 12-frame small trick performance beside Vanessa. She smiles and encourages him, making only small necessary hand gestures, with stationary planted feet.
0: Vanessa and Jerry standing neutrally side by side;
1: Jerry bends his legs slightly preparing a small jump;
2: Jerry rises, all four paws leaving the ground by about 15px, Vanessa watches;
3: jump apex around 35px above the baseline, Jerry's small legs neatly tucked, same body size;
4: Jerry lands gently on four paws, slight knee bend;
5: Jerry lowers his hindquarters to sit;
6: Jerry sits upright with perked ears and a small tail wag;
7: seated Jerry lifts ONE front paw toward Vanessa as she lowers an encouraging hand toward him;
8: Jerry's raised paw gently touches her offered hand, both remain clearly separate, no fused limbs;
9: Jerry lowers the paw while still sitting, Vanessa returns her hand toward rest;
10: Jerry rises from sitting, halfway to standing;
11: both in the same neutral standing posture as frame 0.
The full jump, sitting gesture and give-paw trick must be visibly distinct and connected by coherent intermediate poses. Keep four dog legs anatomically correct, no floating duplicated paw. His apparent smaller height when sitting is a posture change, not rescaling. The final and initial standing frames match for a clean endpoint.

